questions.question:
The fix proposed in review:

submissions.add(1, {
  route: req.route,                                // "/submissions"
  method: req.method,                              // always "POST" on this handler
  status_class: `${Math.floor(status / 100)}xx`,   // "2xx" ... "5xx"
});
if (req.userId) {
  analytics.track({ name: "submission_created", userId: req.userId, properties: { challengeId: req.challengeId, status } });
}

The admin page "Top submitters this week" now reads the submission_created events from the analytics warehouse.

Statement: "After this change codedev_submissions_total stays at a handful of series however many learners submit, and the per-learner question is answered from the events, which are built to hold one row per submission."

challenges.description:
The request sent to an AI coding assistant was: "Add a metric so we can see which learners submit the most." The assistant added a counter, codedev_submissions_total, to the submit handler (POST /submissions) with the labels user_id, url (the raw URL with its query string), method and status (the exact code), and a topLearners(n) for the admin dashboard that sums the counter by user_id. The totals were right, the tests passed and the pull request was merged. Three weeks later the metrics bill had tripled: the counter had one series per learner, per challenge, per query string and per status code, and it grew with every new learner.

Fix SubmissionMetrics:
- the counter keeps only bounded labels: route (the route template matched by the router, req.route), method, and status_class (the first digit of the response status + "xx", for example "4xx");
- per-learner counts go to the analytics sink provided in the file: for each submission by a signed-in learner, call analytics.track({ name: "submission_created", userId, properties: { challengeId, status } }); a request without a userId (an expired session answered with 401) is counted in the metric but not sent to analytics;
- topLearners(n) answers from the sink with analytics.topUsers("submission_created", n) (highest count first, ties by learner id).

Input (stdin, JSON): a list of steps: { "submit": { "userId": "u_an", "challengeId": "c_17", "status": 202, "query": "&attempt=2" } } (userId null without a session), { "simulate": { "learners": 10000, "perLearner": 2, "challenges": 40 } }, { "total": true }, { "top": 3 }, { "series": true } and { "analytics": true }. The runner prints the total of the counter ("total: 6"), the top list ("top 3: u_an 3, u_chi 2, u_binh 1"), the number of series of the counter (and the series themselves when there are at most 12) and the submission_created events in the sink. Do not change the types, FakeMeter, AnalyticsSink or the test runner. The public tests pass on the assistant's code; the hidden tests count the series with 10,000 learners and check what reaches the analytics sink.
